figdown 0.1 bitfield
# `DRAWN-ANNOTATION-FORM`: `note=` IS LIVE AGAIN, LANGUAGE-WIDE. What this fixture
# pins has CHANGED SIDES — it is no longer a RETIREMENT, it is a REFUSAL.
#
# `DESCRIPTION-KEY-SPELLING` retired the spelling in order to RESERVE the name for the
# drawn callout `ANNOTATION-LOCATOR-SPLIT` had measured, and SYNTAX-STYLE RULE 4.9 lets that
# reservation be cashed: a spelling retired before the first stable release,
# where no published document carried the old meaning, may return in its own
# namespace. So `note=` is now the DRAWN annotation and is accepted on
# `node`, `process`, `decision`, `terminator`, `state`, `group`, `edge`,
# `flowline`, `transition` and `title` under `figdown 0.3` (cases 800-802),
# and the retirement diagnostic this file used to pin is GONE from the engine.
#
# `field` still refuses the key, and NOT because the spelling was renamed. It
# refuses on EVIDENCE: the bitfield genre already carries `description=` for
# prose a machine reads, and no measured figure needs a DRAWN per-field aside,
# so granting one directive both keys would spend the AUDIENCE distinction
# before anything needs it. The refusal is version-independent — this document
# still declares `figdown 0.1` precisely because raising the header to 0.3
# would NOT make line 20 legal (case 803 pins both halves of that).
#
# The file keeps its name. Renaming it would break every citation of it by
# path in files this change does not otherwise touch, and STATUS.txt already
# records what a rename costs the suite; case 014 kept its name through the
# same kind of shift for the same reason.
bitfield hdr "Header" word=32 numbering=msb0
field "A" 16 note="the key field refuses"
field "B" 16 description="the key field takes"
